    "slug": "build-a-simple-icp-scoring-framework-for-b2b-outbound",
    "title": "Build a Simple ICP Scoring Framework for B2B Outbound",
    "description": "Learn how to build a point-based ICP scoring framework to prioritize B2B leads. Includes a weighting template for firmographics, intent, and persona fit.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-18",
    "content": "An **ICP scoring framework** is a quantitative method for ranking prospects based on their alignment with your ideal customer profile and their likelihood to convert. By assigning point values to specific firmographic, technographic, and behavioral data points, teams can objectively prioritize high-value accounts over generic leads.\n\n## The Problem With Subjective Qualification\n\nMost small sales teams rely on gut feeling. A founder or early sales hire looks at a company name and decides if it feels right. This leads to inconsistent results and wasted capacity. Without a standardized **lead scoring** system, your outbound efforts are distributed evenly across high-fit and low-fit targets, effectively diluting your ROI.\n\nWhen you scale from ten emails a day to one thousand, subjectivity becomes a bottleneck. You need a data-driven model that tells you exactly who to contact first. This is where an **account scoring** model bridges the gap between a broad market and a targeted list.\n\n## The Three Pillars of a B2B ICP Scoring Framework\n\nTo build a functional framework, you must weigh three distinct categories of data. For teams using a lean stack, these categories should be measurable through standard data providers like LinkedIn or Apollo.\n\n### 1. Firmographic Fit (40% of Score)\nFirmographics represent the baseline requirements for a company to derive value from your product. If they do not meet these criteria, they should be disqualified immediately, regardless of other factors.\n\n- **Industry:** Does their sector align with your existing case studies?\n- **Employee Count:** Are they small enough to be agile but large enough to afford you?\n- **Revenue:** Is there a budget allocated for your category of software?\n- **Geography:** Can you legally and operationally serve their region?\n\n### 2. Technographic and Intent Data (30% of Score)\nTechnographics identify the tools a company already uses. If you integrate with Salesforce, a company using Salesforce is worth more than one using a niche CRM. Intent data indicates they are currently looking for a solution.\n\n- **Complementary Stack:** They use tools that make your product more effective.\n- **Competitive Stack:** They use a direct competitor (a signal of category awareness).\n- **Job Postings:** Hiring for roles that your product supports or replaces.\n\n### 3. Persona Fit (30% of Score)\nEven a perfect company is a dead end if you reach out to the wrong individual. **B2B qualification** at the lead level ensures you are talking to a decision-maker or a high-leverage champion.\n\n- **Job Title:** Does their seniority match your typical buyer?\n- **Department:** Are they in the specific functional group that feels the pain point?\n- **Time in Role:** New hires often have a mandate to change processes and buy new software.\n\n## The Recommended Weighting Template\n\nYou can implement this point system inside any CRM or spreadsheet. Assign points to each attribute, then sum them to create a total lead score. Use the following table as a starting point for your **icp scoring** calculations.\n\n| Category | Attribute | Points | Weighting Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Firmographic | Tier 1 Industry | +30 | High conversion historicals |\n| Firmographic | Employee Count (50-200) | +20 | Ideal mid-market sweet spot |\n| Technographic | Uses Competitor X | +15 | High awareness, switch play |\n| Persona | Head of Sales / VP Sales | +20 | Direct decision-maker |\n| Persona | Junior Individual Contributor | -10 | Low leverage, avoid for initial outreach |\n| Intent | Actively hiring for Sales Ops | +15 | Signal of scale and process pain |\n\n## Implementing the Framework Without Manual Data Entry\n\nThe traditional way to execute this is manual. A sales operations manager builds a complex formula in a CRM, pulls data from a provider like Apollo, and cleans it in a spreadsheet. This is a high-friction process that most 1-10 person teams cannot sustain. \n\nWhen you use a platform like [OutReign](https://outreign.io), this entire **icp scoring framework** is automated. Instead of manually assigning points to individual rows, you describe your ICP in plain English. The system handles the ranking and verification, ensuring your team only spends time on prospects with the highest point potential. \n\n## Why Scoring Beats Filtering\n\nFiltering is binary; a prospect is either in or out. Scoring is nuanced. By using an **icp scoring** model, you can segment your outbound campaigns based on heat. \n\n- **Score 80-100:** High-touch, highly personalized manual emails.\n- **Score 50-79:** Automated sequences with light personalization.\n- **Score below 50:** Low-priority leads for long-term nurturing or exclusion.\n\nThis tiered approach ensures that your most expensive resource—your time—is allocated to the prospects most likely to close. You can read more about how this compares to traditional tools in our [Apollo vs Instantly vs Salesloft guide](/blog/apollo-vs-instantly-vs-salesloft).\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nAn **icp scoring framework** removes the guesswork from outbound sales. By quantifying firmographic, technographic, and persona fit, you transform your lead list into a prioritized roadmap. Start with the weights provided above, track your conversion rates, and adjust the points every quarter based on which leads actually turn into revenue.\n\nIf you want to automate this entire workflow without managing three different software subscriptions, see how we do it at [outreign.io](https://outreign.io).",
